    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, the \"probes\" idea helps. so it's basically checking for resonance or overlap. but what happens if the signal is super chaotic or has these weird, sudden disruptions? does the math still work, or does the \"fingerprint\" just become impossible to read because there's too much noise or interference to separate the real signal from the junk?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Chaotic signals or sudden disruptions—like a sharp spike or a burst of noise—are precisely what the Fourier Transform illuminates, though perhaps not in the neat, clean way you might hope. These abrupt changes are characterized by very high-frequency content, which the transform will capture as broad, energetic spikes in the frequency domain. If the signal is *too* random, like pure white noise, the resulting \"fingerprint\" will be spread out evenly across *all* frequencies, which is the mathematical signature of randomness.\n\nThis is where practical signal processing, like filtering, comes in—it's like using a specialized sieve to separate the useful ingredients from the chaotic dust. You use knowledge of what you *expect* the signal to look like to carve out the relevant frequency bands.",
      "source": "target",
